{
  "_comment": "OPTIONAL — only for the Premiere Pro finishing lane (the premiere-pro skill). EASY PATH: run ./setup-premiere.sh — it installs the pinned engine + the panel and writes .mcp.json for your OS automatically (macOS and Windows both fully supported — on Windows run it from Git Bash, and the bridge folder is %TEMP%\\premiere-mcp-bridge instead of /tmp; plain Linux has no Premiere). This file is only the reference for what setup-premiere.sh writes, or for wiring by hand. Engine is a pinned local clone of github.com/hetpatel-11/Adobe_Premiere_Pro_MCP (do NOT use the premiere-pro-mcp npm package — it's an abandoned republish with a broken CEP bridge shim). Manual steps if you skip the script: 1) git clone https://github.com/hetpatel-11/Adobe_Premiere_Pro_MCP.git vendor/premiere-mcp && cd vendor/premiere-mcp && npm install && npm run build  2) make the panel start its own bridge, BEFORE copying it: uv run lanes/premiere/premiere-templates/apply-autostart-patch.py vendor/premiere-mcp/cep-plugin (idempotent)  3) install the panel: rm -rf \"$HOME/Library/Application Support/Adobe/CEP/extensions/MCPBridgeCEP\" && cp -R cep-plugin \"$HOME/Library/Application Support/Adobe/CEP/extensions/MCPBridgeCEP\" (enable CEP debug mode once if the panel doesn't appear: defaults write com.adobe.CSXS.12 PlayerDebugMode 1)  4) copy this file to .mcp.json (the args path resolves relative to the project root)  5) restart Claude Code and answer yes when it asks to enable this project's MCP servers  6) restart Premiere; the patched panel starts the bridge itself (only an unpatched panel needs Window > Extensions > MCP Bridge (CEP) > Start Bridge). If you don't use Premiere, do nothing — leave this as .example.",
  "mcpServers": {
    "premiere-pro": {
      "command": "node",
      "args": ["vendor/premiere-mcp/dist/index.js"],
      "env": {
        "PREMIERE_TEMP_DIR": "/tmp/premiere-mcp-bridge",
        "PREMIERE_TIMEOUT_MS": "60000",
        "PREMIERE_MCP_TELEMETRY": "0"
      }
    }
  }
}
